# Line endings are decided here rather than by each machine's git configuration.
# A .gitattributes entry outranks core.autocrlf and core.eol, so the rule
# travels with the repository instead of depending on who cloned it.
#
#   text    normalise to LF on the way in, whatever the working tree held.
#   eol=lf  on the way out, write what the index holds. Git only ever adds CR
#           (for eol=crlf) and never strips it, so a blob that predates this
#           rule keeps the endings it was committed with.
#
# LF is also what the format asks for in stored files: "The standard character
# encoding is UTF-8 without BOM, with LF line endings." A parser still accepts
# CRLF and a lone CR — this is about what the repository holds, not about what
# setay can read.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe walks a batch file by byte offset and can lose a label or cut a block
# short when the endings are LF alone, so these are written out with CRLF and
# the shell reading them gets what it expects. Neither extension appears in the
# tree today; the rule is here so that the first one added is already covered.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
